            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">July 9, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">108</td><td class="px-4 py-3">91</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">August 6, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">102</td><td class="px-4 py-3">183</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">September 10, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">99</td><td class="px-4 py-3">182</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">October 8, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">99</td><td class="px-4 py-3">86</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Source: WelcomeBC invitations to apply page, last updated October 8, 2026.</p>

      <p>
        The pattern is clear: draws came roughly once a month, the minimum score fell 16 points between May and September, and it held at 99 in October with a smaller draw. A plausible reading is that B.C. worked through its backlog of high-scoring ECE registrations over the summer. Keep in mind that each invitation is valid for only 30 days, and your registration leaves the pool once you are invited, whether or not you apply.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Two worked examples</h2>

      <p>
        These are illustrations using the BC PNP's 200-point scoring grid. Your own numbers will differ; use our <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP calculator</a> to check yours.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Example 1: Five Year ECE in Kelowna</h3>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">3 years of ECE experience</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right">12</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">At least 1 year of that experience in Canada</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right">10</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Currently working full-time for the supporting employer</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right">10</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">ECE diploma (post-secondary) completed in B.C.</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right">5 + 8</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Professional designation (Five Year certificate)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right">5</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 6 English</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right">15</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Wage of $26.00 an hour</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right">11</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Area 3 (outside Metro Vancouver and the five Area 2 municipalities)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right">15</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Regional experience (1 year working outside Metro Vancouver)</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right">10</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-green-50 font-bold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Total</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-right">101</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        At 101, this candidate would have cleared the 99 cutoff in the last two draws. Notice how much comes from location: the 25 regional points are a quarter of the total.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Example 2: One Year ECE in Burnaby, trained abroad</h3>

      <p>
        Two years of ECE experience (8), one year in Canada (10), currently working for the employer (10), a foreign ECE diploma (5), professional designation (5), CLB 7 (20), a wage of $24.00 an hour (9) and Area 1 (0) gives <strong>67 points</strong>, 32 below the recent cutoff. Raising English to CLB 9 adds 10 points; a bachelor's degree instead of a diploma would add another 10. Even then, this candidate stays below 99. For ECEs in Metro Vancouver, the honest message is that the Childcare draw is hard to reach without very strong language scores, B.C. education or a higher wage, and a job in Area 2 or 3 changes the picture more than anything else.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Step by step</h2>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Get certified.</strong> Apply through My ECE Registry for a One Year or Five Year certificate. If you have only an Assistant certificate, plan the coursework to upgrade.</li>
        <li><strong>Secure a qualifying job.</strong> Full-time, indeterminate, with a licensed centre that meets the employee-count and one-year rules.</li>
        <li><strong>Take a language test.</strong> CLB 4 is the minimum, but every CLB level from 4 to 9 adds 5 points.</li>
        <li><strong>Register.</strong> Registration is free and lasts up to 12 months. If you qualify for a federal Express Entry program, consider the EEBC option (ECE is also in the federal education category, although IRCC held no education rounds in 2026).</li>
        <li><strong>Apply within 30 days of an invitation</strong> and pay the $1,750 application fee. WelcomeBC says about 80% of applications are decided within three months.</li>
        <li><strong>Apply to IRCC for permanent residence</strong> after nomination. See <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-after-nomination" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">what happens after nomination</a>.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What to expect in 2027 (analysis)</h2>

      <p>
        B.C. has not announced its 2027 allocation or priorities. What we can say from the record:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>ECEs remain a named priority.</strong> As of October 14, 2026, WelcomeBC still lists NOC 42202 under Care. We expect Childcare draws to continue into 2027 if the Care framework stays, but that is an expectation, not an announcement.</li>
        <li><strong>The cutoff depends on supply and allocation.</strong> B.C. had 6,254 nominations for 2026 after a federal top-up in August. If the 2027 allocation is similar, monthly ECE draws of roughly the 2026 size are plausible; a smaller allocation would likely mean fewer invitations and higher cutoffs.</li>
        <li><strong>Watch the federal levels plan.</strong> The 2027–2029 plan, expected in early November 2026, will set the national PNP target that B.C.'s allocation comes from.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Is this pathway right for you?</h2>

      <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
        <div class="bg-green-50 border border-green-200 p-6 rounded-2xl">
          <p class="font-bold text-green-900 mb-2">Strong fit</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-green-800 space-y-2 text-base">
            <li>One Year or Five Year ECEs already working full-time in a licensed centre</li>
            <li>ECEs outside Metro Vancouver</li>
            <li>Graduates of B.C. ECE programs (8 extra education points)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl">
          <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Harder fit</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-slate-700 space-y-2 text-base">
            <li>ECE Assistants (no targeted invitation)</li>
            <li>Staff of very small or unlicensed operations</li>
            <li>Workers with under two years of skilled experience</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Common mistakes</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Registering with an expired or Assistant-level certificate.</strong> A targeted invitation issued on a certificate you do not hold leads to refusal.</li>
        <li><strong>Letting the One Year certificate lapse</strong> before completing the 500 hours.</li>
        <li><strong>Overstating wages</strong> by folding in payments the BC PNP does not count. The guide warns that inflated wages lead to refusal.</li>
        <li><strong>Claiming a different work location</strong> from where you actually report to work. Area points follow your real location.</li>
        <li><strong>Assuming the employer qualifies.</strong> Check the employee count and licence before your employer signs the declaration form.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        For families and caregivers more broadly, see our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-caregivers-childcare" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">caregivers and childcare guide</a>, and for how ECEs fit within all Care occupations, our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-care-healthcare-occupations-list-2027" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Care occupations list for 2027</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 not-prose">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
